   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved comprehensive programmes to facilitate employment for just transitions in the environmental and digital dimensions in urban and rural areas, targeting vulnerable workers, by the end of 2025</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">2024&#x2013;2025, Brazil navigates a complex global scenario marked by geopolitical tensions, climate crises, and inflation, which intensify labour market vulnerabilities and social inequalities. Domestically, political fragmentation and fiscal limits challenge structural reforms. After 2.9% growth in 2023, the economy expanded 3.4% in 2024, with 2025 projected at 2.4%. Employment reached 106.8 million, and unemployment averaged 6.2%. Yet informality remains high&#x2014;nearly 39 million workers&#x2014;and average real wages (BRL 3,294 or USD 660) remain insufficient, especially for youth, women, and marginalized groups.

In this context, the ILO Office for Brazil is intensifying efforts to promote decent work and social justice through an integrated strategy that supports just transitions in environmental and digital spheres.

In 2024&#x2013;2025, it works with partners to foster youth employment, skills development, social protection, and labour formalization. Efforts include implementing youth inclusion policies and preparing a national pact for green jobs in sectors like renewable energy and the bioeconomy.

The ILO also supports vocational training to align apprenticeships with future labour market needs.
Labour formalization and social protection are central, with technical support for new regulatory frameworks for platform workers and strengthening of inclusive, gender-responsive public employment services.

The Office contributes to Brazil&#x2019;s Ecological Transformation Plan, focusing on sustainable finance, energy transition, and bioeconomy, and promotes gender-sensitive data on green jobs and dialogue with Indigenous Peoples (C169).

The ILO also supports trade unions and academia in improving platform worker rights and helps modernize the apprenticeship system to address digital skills gaps. Despite challenges, Brazil&#x2019;s leadership at COP30 and in the Global Coalition for Social Justice offers momentum to advance the decent work agenda with equity and sustainabilty.</narrative>
  </description>
